    "text": "Rinse first, then clean · Use the right tools for coated lenses · Storage matters when you are not wearing them · Water first · Case every day · Protect your coatings · Caring for Your Glasses · Simple care habits that protect lenses, coatings, and day-to-day visual clarity. · Lens Care Guide · Small cleaning habits have a big impact on how long your lenses stay clear and comfortable. Good lens care protects coatings, reduces scratches, and helps your glasses perform the way they were designed to. · Dust and grit can scratch lenses if you rub them dry. A quick rinse with lukewarm water before using approved lens cleaner helps lift debris away before wiping. · Microfiber cloths and lens-safe cleaning sprays are designed to clean without stripping or scuffing coatings. Paper towels, shirt hems, tissues, and household cleaners can shorten the life of premium coatings. · A protective case reduces accidental scratches, frame warping, and coating wear. Heat, dashboards, and steamy environments can also stress lens materials and treatments over time. · Rinsing before wiping reduces the chance of rubbing abrasive particles across the lens surface. · Most avoidable scratches happen when glasses are loose in a bag, pocket, or on a hard surface. · Premium anti-reflective and easy-clean layers last longer when cleaned gently and consistently. · Can I use glass cleaner or alcohol wipes on my lenses? · It is better to avoid household cleaners unless your optician specifically approves them. Some chemicals can damage coatings or frame finishes. · Why do my lenses still smear after cleaning? · Skin oils, old cloth buildup, or using the wrong cleaner can leave residue. Washing the microfiber cloth regularly also helps. · Is hot water bad for glasses? · Yes, very hot water can stress coatings and some frame materials. Stick with lukewarm water instead. · How often should I replace microfiber cloths? · Replace them when they stay dirty, feel rough, or no longer clean well. A worn cloth can reintroduce residue and debris. · Learn About Lens Coatings · Find a Retailer · What to know · Quick takeaways · Common questions · Want help applying this to your routine? · Bring your screen time, driving, reading, and outdoor habits to your next appointment so your optician can recommend the best combination."
